G6a-11 static multi-task syncer and identity isolation validation, 2026-09-08
Base commit: ff2e6fe (G6a-9 lifecycle and metrics)
Branch: dev
Working-tree base: G6a-10 standalone syncer changes, not yet committed.
Environment: local Darwin development workspace; Linux amd64 cross-compilation only.

Scope:
- One standalone process manages 1-8 named tasks with separate journals, cold
  metadata, status and metrics. Legacy single-task configuration remains supported.
- All backend endpoints remain loopback, distinct and operator-owned; each task
  requires an exclusive cold DB. No shared cold DB or distributed ownership claim.
- No remote deployment, service restart, real Redis/Kvrocks multi-backend test,
  business traffic changes, throughput measurement or 4/24-hour stability run.
- Controlled RESP fixtures exercise protocol behavior; they are not real databases.

Implementation and review coverage:
- Reject mixed legacy/named configuration, duplicate or nested/aliased journal
  directories, endpoint reuse, invalid task IDs and excessive declared budgets.
- Named v2 manifests bind task ID, canonical source and cold endpoint before
  opening/recovering data segments or connecting to backends. Legacy manifests
  remain unchanged and cannot be adopted as named journals, even when empty.
- Named checkpoint/pending/anchor operations use task-scoped cold metadata;
  opening a cold DB rejects foreign reserved metadata rather than adopting it.
- Verify identity before segment apply, drain, prune and orphan cleanup.
- Three tasks start concurrently; one terminal failure leaves siblings running
  and records degraded state. Shutdown cancels all before joining all tasks.
- Per-task final state, fixed error categories and task_id metrics are redacted.
- Fix CheckCredentials concurrent access to service task completion state by
  copying task pointers under the service mutex before credential checks.
- Open journal files with O_NONBLOCK and O_NOFOLLOW before private regular-file
  validation; a replaced FIFO manifest cannot block open indefinitely.

Targeted identity regression:
  go test ./internal/gateway -run 'Test(SyncTaskIdentity|IsolatedSyncNamed)' -count=1 -timeout=90s
  PASS 1.177s

Metadata and protocol regression:
  go test -race ./internal/gateway -run 'TestSyncMetadata|TestSyncColdScan|TestSyncPending|TestSyncApplyPlan' -count=3 -timeout=120s
  PASS 4.230s
  Includes legacy and two named task IDs across bootstrap, pending apply, resume,
  prune, foreign metadata rejection and bounded SCAN checks.

Service repeated race regression:
  go test -race ./internal/syncer -count=5 -timeout=120s
  PASS 2.218s

CLI/process repeated race regression:
  go test -race ./cmd/syncer -count=3 -timeout=120s
  PASS 14.966s
  The three-task case starts a real test-binary child using mainWithSignals,
  observes all three PSYNC requests before SIGTERM, joins all three source
  connections, checks all final task logs, and restarts the same three journals.
  Manifest and LOCK inode identities are preserved across the restart.
  Three runs cover six child starts/stops for this multi-task case, in addition
  to the legacy process test. All children and fixture connections are joined.
  Fixtures provide no RDB and accept no business writes: this proves signal,
  concurrent task and empty-journal reopen behavior, NOT data resume correctness.

Final combined race regression (including nonblocking manifest test):
